      "id": 1,
      "text": "Inavale’s name means “in a vale,” reflecting its location in the scenic Republican River valley.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inavale%2C_Nebraska"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Inavale sprang in the early 1870s when homesteaders turned Nebraska’s prairie into thriving farms.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/inavale_nebraska"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town’s post office opened April 3, 1873, becoming the first hub for mail and social life.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/inavale_nebraska"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The post office closed in 1994, reflecting a mid‑century shift toward larger towns amid agricultural consolidation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/inavale_nebraska"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The 1930s Dust Bowl devastated Inavale’s farms, with drought and dust storms crippling harvests and livelihoods.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
